📄 Asset Lookup via Rate-Limited Paginated API (Design Doc)

1. Problem Summary

We are given a paid GET API with strict rate limits (1 call / second).
The API is paginated using updateId, not page numbers.

Goal:
Find whether a specific assetId exists in the API responses by continuously polling the API until:

the asset is found, or

the end of available data is reached.



---

2. API Characteristics

Request Parameters

Param	Description

updateId	Cursor / reference point for pagination
limit	Max number of records per call (e.g., 1000)
apiKey	Authentication key


Example Call

GET /api?updateId=0&limit=1000&apiKey=XYZ


---

3. Response Structure (XML)

Header Section

<header>
  <streamData>
    <nextUpdateId>12345</nextUpdateId>
    <maxUpdateId>99999</maxUpdateId>
  </streamData>
</header>

nextUpdateId → use this for the next API call

maxUpdateId → signals end of stream



---

Data Section

<programAvailabilities>
   ...
   <assetId>ASSET_ABC_123</assetId>
   ...
</programAvailabilities>

We must regex match or parse XML to check if the target assetId exists.


---

4. Core Algorithm (High Level)

startUpdateId = 0

WHILE (true):
    call API with (startUpdateId, limit)
    parse XML response

    IF assetId found:
        stop execution
        return success

    IF nextUpdateId >= maxUpdateId:
        stop execution
        return "asset not found"

    startUpdateId = nextUpdateId
    wait 1 second


---

5. Rate Limiting Strategy

Why Required?

API allows only 1 call per second

Violating this can:

burn quota

block API key



Solution

Use a fixed delay loop:

setTimeout

setInterval

or await sleep(1000)



---

6. JavaScript (Node.js) – Suggested Architecture

Modules

Module	Responsibility

apiClient.js	Makes HTTP GET call
xmlParser.js	Parses XML → JS object
assetMatcher.js	Regex or direct assetId search
poller.js	Loop + rate limiting
index.js	Entry point



---

7. Pseudo Code (JS-Style)

let currentUpdateId = 0;
const LIMIT = 1000;
const TARGET_ASSET_ID = "ASSET_ABC_123";

while (true) {
  const responseXml = await callApi(currentUpdateId, LIMIT);
  
  const {
    nextUpdateId,
    maxUpdateId,
    hasAsset
  } = parseAndCheck(responseXml, TARGET_ASSET_ID);

  if (hasAsset) {
    console.log("Asset found!");
    break;
  }

  if (nextUpdateId >= maxUpdateId) {
    console.log("Reached end. Asset not found.");
    break;
  }

  currentUpdateId = nextUpdateId;
  await sleep(1000); // rate limiting
}


