8. XML Handling Strategy

Option 1 (Recommended): XML → JS Object

Use libraries like:

xml2js

fast-xml-parser


Pros

Clean structure

Less error-prone


Cons

Slight overhead



---

Option 2: Regex Search (Only for assetId)

const regex = new RegExp(`<assetId>${targetId}</assetId>`);
regex.test(xmlString);

Pros

Faster

Simple


Cons

Fragile if XML structure changes


➡️ Hybrid approach recommended:

parse header properly

regex match assetId only



---

9. Error Handling

Scenario	Action

API failure (5xx)	Retry after delay
Invalid XML	Log + retry
Rate limit error	Backoff (2–5 seconds)
nextUpdateId missing	Stop execution



---

10. Exit Conditions (Very Important)

The script must terminate when:

1. Asset is found


2. nextUpdateId >= maxUpdateId


3. Critical unrecoverable error occurs




---

11. Logging (Mandatory)

Log per iteration:

[INFO] Calling API with updateId=12345
[INFO] Asset not found, moving to nextUpdateId=13345

Useful for:

debugging

audit

quota analysis



---

12. Possible Enhancements (Future)

Persist lastUpdateId to disk (resume support)

Exponential backoff on failures

Parallel search not allowed due to rate limit

Config via .env



---

13. Final Recommendation

✅ Node.js + async/await + XML parser + 1s delay loop
This is the safest, quota-friendly, and maintainable approach.

